# Near-minimal. One deliberate exception, below.
#
# Everything else in this project is tracked on purpose. An over-broad ignore
# file is the likeliest reason for content going missing between machines — if a
# file belongs to the project, it belongs in the repo.

# ============================================================================
# RAW CLIENT ASSETS — NOT IN THE REPO. READ THIS BEFORE CLONING.
# ============================================================================
# _assets/ holds ~247 files / ~147 MB of the client's original high-resolution
# booklet photography, organised as _assets/{range}/{slug}/.
#
# Excluded to keep the repo light — a decision taken 27 Aug 2026.
#
# ⚠️ CONSEQUENCE: a fresh clone does NOT get these files, and the derived
# web-sized images in _deploy/img/ cannot be regenerated without them. This is
# the same failure mode that lost _archive/data and _tools between machines
# earlier in this project.
#
# So the raw assets MUST live somewhere backed up that is not this repo —
# shared drive, the client's original zips, or Git LFS. Whoever clones this
# needs them copied across separately before running _tools/optimise-assets.ps1.
#
# What IS tracked: the derived, web-sized images in _deploy/img/. The site
# builds and deploys correctly from a clone without _assets/ — only
# re-optimising requires the originals.
_assets/

# OS cruft
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
.DS_Store

# Editor / IDE local state
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~

# Local scratch only
*.log

# Mac zip junk (the client's asset drops arrive with these)
__MACOSX/
._*

# Build tooling and output.
# Added with the same caution as the note at the top of this file: these three
# are reproducible from the repo, so losing them between machines costs a
# `npm install` and a `npm run build` — nothing else. Everything that is NOT
# reproducible stays tracked.
node_modules/
/dist/
/_deploy/dist/
